WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.675
Episode seven: studies.

2
00:00:01.675 --> 00:00:11.802
This video assumes APEX installed with OPM Flow, as in episode one, and a terminal in the APEX folder.

3
00:00:11.802 --> 00:00:14.893
Today we compare optimizers in a study.

4
00:00:14.893 --> 00:00:16.637
APEX ports PET's popt.

5
00:00:16.637 --> 00:00:28.644
The case: examples/optimization/3well/enopt.toml is PET's popt tutorial.

6
00:00:28.644 --> 00:00:44.625
Its objective is simulated, so it needs a deck simulator that APEX supports (opm_flow, eclipse): here OPM Flow, as in episode one.

7
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:48.001
Controls: the values the optimizer changes.

8
00:00:48.001 --> 00:01:00.371
Each has its bounds.

9
00:01:00.371 --> 00:01:03.810
The objective is the net present value, NPV.

10
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:15.725
The page states it as -NPV in millions: minimising it maximises NPV.

11
00:01:15.725 --> 00:01:17.352
The case uses enopt.

12
00:01:17.352 --> 00:01:23.208
APEX provides ensemble optimizers (enopt, genopt, smcopt), among others.

13
00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:24.591
Each is a plugin.

14
00:01:24.591 --> 00:01:27.519
Which of two is better on this case?

15
00:01:27.519 --> 00:01:35.827
A study answers that.

16
00:01:35.827 --> 00:01:40.873
A study of optimizations compares optimizers and settings over seeds.

17
00:01:40.873 --> 00:01:45.773
A cell is one run: one case, method and variant, for one replicate.

18
00:01:45.773 --> 00:02:08.545
Save it as study.toml in examples/optimization/3well, next to enopt.toml: a study's case paths are relative to the study file's folder.

19
00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:09.676
Three replicates.

20
00:02:09.676 --> 00:02:28.180
The first replicate uses the case's own seed, so it reproduces a plain apex optimize of that case; the others are derived from [study] seed, so the study is reproducible.

21
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.176
The case is the three-well configuration.

22
00:02:31.176 --> 00:02:46.554
The methods are complete [optimizer] sections: enopt and smcopt.

23
00:02:46.554 --> 00:02:49.062
Two variants: base, and cheap water.

24
00:02:49.062 --> 00:03:05.195
A variant patches anything but [optimizer]: here, one option of the objective.

25
00:03:05.195 --> 00:03:19.276
The study's metrics takes only what the comparison reports: here, the final and the best objective, and the evaluations used.

26
00:03:19.276 --> 00:03:24.355
apex study run submits the study's runs as background jobs and returns at once.

27
00:03:24.355 --> 00:03:43.802
The jobs run with or without the job service: with uv run apex service start running, the service schedules them; without it, the command starts the first ones and each job starts the next as it ends.

28
00:03:43.802 --> 00:03:46.193
Now we run it, from the APEX folder.

29
00:03:46.193 --> 00:04:03.267
apex study run takes the study file; status and compare take the study's name, its [study] name.

30
00:04:03.267 --> 00:04:06.191
Either way, apex study status follows them.

31
00:04:06.191 --> 00:04:07.959
It takes the study's name.

32
00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:09.999
Look at the state of each run.

33
00:04:09.999 --> 00:04:26.846
Every run is an OPM Flow optimization, so this takes a while.

34
00:04:26.846 --> 00:04:30.084
We cut the wait; its real length is in the description.

35
00:04:30.084 --> 00:04:46.140
The same command again: every run should now be finished.

36
00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:53.168
The comparison has each method and variant's mean and spread over the replicates, and paired differences with Wilcoxon tests.

37
00:04:53.168 --> 00:05:16.848
Find the final objective for each method and variant.

38
00:05:16.848 --> 00:05:22.348
Thank you for watching.
